.TH CDIR 1 "October 2026" "cdir 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cdir, c \- directory launcher with named bookmarks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B c \-i
.RI [ NAME | PATH ]
.br
.B c \-a
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-m ]
.I NAME
.RI [ PATH ]
.br
.B c \-d
.IR NAME ...
.br
.B c \-l
.br
.B c
.BR \-h " | " \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cdir
is
.BR cd (1)
with a memory and a visual directory picker.
.B c napkin
jumps to a saved directory;
.B c
alone opens a keyboard-driven, Dired-inspired browser starting in the
current directory.
.PP
A program cannot change the working directory of the shell that started
it. So
.B cdir
only
.I prints
the chosen directory on standard output, and the shell function
.BR c ,
installed by the shell integration (see
.BR "SHELL INTEGRATION" ),
captures it and runs
.B cd
in your shell. Running
.B cdir
directly is useful in scripts, e.g.
.BR "dir=$(cdir notes)" .
.PP
When
.B cdir
prints a directory, standard output contains exactly that absolute path
followed by one newline, and nothing else. The interactive interface is
drawn on
.IR /dev/tty ;
errors go to standard error.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B c
Browse interactively from the current directory.
.TP
.BI c " NAME"
Go to bookmark
.IR NAME .
No interface is started. If there is no exact match but exactly one
bookmark starts with
.IR NAME ,
that one is used. If several bookmarks match by prefix, or none does but
some match fuzzily, a small picker lists the candidates. An exact match
always wins.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive " [\fINAME\fR|\fIPATH\fR]"
Browse, starting from a bookmark or a path instead of the current
directory. An argument containing a slash, or starting with
.RB \(lq . \(rq
or
.RB \(lq ~ \(rq,
is always a path.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add " \fINAME\fR [\fIPATH\fR]"
Bookmark
.I PATH
(default: the current directory) as
.IR NAME .
The path is made absolute; a leading
.BR ~ ,
.B $HOME
or
.B ${HOME}
is expanded if the shell did not already do so.
.B ..
components are resolved lexically, as
.B cd
does, and symbolic links are kept as written. The directory must exist.
If
.I NAME
already exists with a different path,
.B cdir
asks before replacing it; without a terminal to ask on, it refuses.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
With
.BR \-a :
replace an existing bookmark without asking.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-allow\-missing
With
.BR \-a :
accept a directory that does not exist yet.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete " \fINAME\fR..."
Delete bookmarks. No confirmation is asked.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List bookmarks with aligned columns. Paths below your home directory are
shown as
.BR ~/... ;
bookmarks whose directory is gone are marked
.BR [missing] ,
but never removed automatically. When standard output is not a terminal,
the list is printed as
.IB NAME \et PATH
lines, with paths escaped as in the bookmark file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.PP
Short options may be combined
.RB ( "c \-af napkin ~/Napkin" ).
.B \-\-
ends option processing.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
The list shows
.B ./
(the directory you are in),
.B ../
and the subdirectories, sorted naturally and case-insensitively. Hidden
directories and files are not shown unless toggled on.
.PP
The model is:
.B Enter
moves you around,
.B Space
picks a destination.
.TP 18
.BR j ", " k ", " \(da ", " \(ua
Move. Also
.BR Ctrl+N / Ctrl+P ,
.BR PgUp / PgDn ;
.BR g / G
or
.BR Home / End
for the first and last entry.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " l ", " \(->
Open the highlighted directory. On
.B ../
go to the parent; on
.B ./
choose the current directory and exit.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " h ", " \(<- ", " \-
Go to the parent directory (the cursor lands on the directory you came
from).
.TP
.BR Space ", " Ctrl+Enter
Choose the highlighted directory and exit. On
.B ./
this is the current directory. Many terminals send the same code for
Ctrl+Enter and Enter;
.B cdir
recognises Ctrl+Enter on terminals that support the kitty keyboard
protocol (e.g. Ghostty, kitty, recent Alacritty, foot, WezTerm) or xterm's
modifyOtherKeys.
.B Ctrl+J
works everywhere.
.TP
.B /
Filter the current directory as you type (fuzzy:
.B prj
finds
.BR Projects ).
.B Enter
opens the best match,
.B Ctrl+J
chooses it,
.B Tab
keeps the filter and returns to normal keys,
.B Esc
clears it. Only the current directory is searched; nothing is scanned
recursively.
.TP
.B a
Bookmark the highlighted directory. You are asked for a name (a
suggestion derived from the directory name is pre-filled).
.TP
.B A
Bookmark the current directory.
.TP
.B b
Open the bookmark list.
.TP
.B ~
Go to your home directory.
.TP
.B .
Show or hide hidden directories (this session only).
.TP
.B f
Show or hide files (dimmed, for orientation only).
.TP
.B ?
Show all keys.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Ctrl+C
Quit without changing directory (exit status 4).
.B Esc
first clears an active filter.
.PP
In the bookmark list:
.TP 18
.BR Enter ", " Space
Go to the highlighted bookmark.
.TP
.BR l ", " \(->
Browse from the highlighted bookmark.
.TP
.B /
Filter bookmark names.
.TP
.B d
Delete the highlighted bookmark (asks first).
.TP
.BR Esc ", " h ", " b
Back to the browser (when started with an ambiguous
.BI "c " NAME\fR,
.B Esc
cancels).
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SH BOOKMARK FILE
Bookmarks live in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cdir/bookmarks ,
by default
.IR ~/.config/cdir/bookmarks ,
created on first use. It is a plain text file you may edit by hand:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# comment
napkin=/home/aragorn/Projects/Napkin
music=/home/aragorn/Music/Live Recordings
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bookmark names consist of letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
and may not start with
.BR \- ,
so they can never be mistaken for options. Since names cannot contain
.BR = ,
everything after the first
.B =
is the path, which must be absolute. In the path,
.B \e\e
stands for a backslash,
.B \en
for a newline and
.B \er
for a carriage return; all other characters, including spaces and tabs,
are literal. If a name appears more than once, the last line wins. Lines
that cannot be parsed are reported by
.B c \-l
and otherwise ignored; they are kept unchanged when
.B cdir
rewrites the file.
.PP
Changes are written to a temporary file which is synced and then renamed
over the bookmark file, so the file is never half-written. Concurrent
writers are serialised with
.BR flock (2)
on
.IR bookmarks.lock .
If the bookmark file is a symbolic link (for example into a dotfiles
repository), the link target is updated and the link is kept.
.SH SYMBOLIC LINKS
.B cdir
treats paths the way the shell's
.B cd
does: symlinks are preserved, not resolved.
.B c \-a x ~/link
stores
.IR ~/link ,
and browsing into a symlinked directory shows and returns the path
through the link. Symlinks to directories are listed with their target;
broken links are only shown (as such) when files are shown. Set
.B follow_symlinks=true
to receive physical paths from the browser instead.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B cdir
works without any configuration. The optional file
.I ~/.config/cdir/config
accepts:
.TP
.BR show_hidden= false | true
Show dot-directories when the browser starts.
.TP
.BR show_files= false | true
List regular files below the directories.
.TP
.BR follow_symlinks= false | true
Print the resolved physical path when choosing in the browser.
.TP
.BR sort= natural | alpha
Natural order ("disc2" before "disc10") or plain alphabetical.
.PP
The environment variable
.B NO_COLOR
disables colours; the interface then uses only bold and reverse video.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
Add one line to your shell's startup file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# ~/.bashrc
source ~/.local/share/cdir/bash.sh

# ~/.zshrc, after compinit
source ~/.local/share/cdir/zsh.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
This defines the function
.B c
and tab completion of bookmark names. When
.B c
selects a directory it runs
.B cdir
in a command substitution, and only if the exit status is 0 does it run
.BR "builtin cd \-\- " "\fIdir\fR."
Commands that do not select a directory
.RB ( \-a ", " \-d ", " \-l ", " \-h ", " \-v )
are run directly so their output is not captured. Directory names with
spaces, tabs, newlines (including trailing ones), a leading dash, or
non-ASCII characters are handled. An existing alias named
.B c
is removed.
.PP
Completion lists bookmark names by running the hidden command
.BR "cdir \-\-names" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for selecting commands, a directory was printed.
.TP
.B 1
General error (unreadable bookmark file, no terminal, refused replace...).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command or arguments, including invalid bookmark names.
.TP
.B 3
Bookmark not found.
.TP
.B 4
Cancelled by the user.
.TP
.B 5
The target directory does not exist or cannot be entered.
.PP
The
.B c
function changes directory only on status 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cdir/bookmarks
Bookmarks.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cdir/config
Optional settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/cdir/bash.sh\fR, \fI~/.local/share/cdir/zsh.sh
Shell integration (for a user-local install).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ c \-a napkin ~/Projects/Napkin
Added bookmark:
napkin \(-> /home/aragorn/Projects/Napkin
$ c napkin
$ pwd
/home/aragorn/Projects/Napkin
$ c nap            # unique prefix
$ c \-i napkin      # browse from the bookmark
$ c \-l
$ c \-af napkin .   # repoint without asking
$ c \-d napkin
$ cp \-r "$(cdir notes)" /backup   # use from scripts
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cd (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR zsh (1)
